.SH NAME
ktm-guide-generics \- Generics (chapter 17 of the ktm language guide)
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Functions, structs and methods can take type parameters: one definition, written once, used with many types. Each use is compiled for its types, producing the same code you would write by hand, with no \fBvoid *\fR and no casts. This chapter covers generic functions, how the types are worked out at a call, generic structs and their methods, methods with type parameters of their own, generic bases, the library's generic containers, and the errors generics give.
.SH Generic functions
.PP
Type parameters go in \fB<...>\fR after the function's name; they can be used anywhere a type can in the parameters, the return type and the body.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
T max<T>(T a, T b) {
    return a > b ? a : b;
}

i32 main() {
    printf("%d\en", max(3, 7));
    printf("%.1f\en", max(1.5, 0.5));
    printf("%c\en", max('a', 'z'));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
7
1.5
z
.EE
.RE
.PP
Each type \fBmax\fR is called with gets its own compiled copy, an instance: \fBmax<i64>\fR, \fBmax<f64>\fR and \fBmax<u8>\fR above. They are as fast as three hand-written functions.
.PP
A generic function can take pointers to its type parameter:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void swap<T>(T *a, T *b) {
    T t = *a;
    *a = *b;
    *b = t;
}

i32 main() {
    i64 x = 1, y = 2;
    swap(&x, &y);
    u8 *s = "left", *t = "right";
    swap(&s, &t);
    printf("%d %d %s %s\en", x, y, s, t);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
2 1 right left
.EE
.RE
.PP
And slices of it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
T sum<T>(T[] xs) {
    T total = 0;
    for (i64 i = 0; i < xs.len; i++) {
        total += xs[i];
    }
    return total;
}

i32 main() {
    i64 ints[3] = {1, 2, 3};
    f64 floats[2] = {0.5, 0.25};
    printf("%d %.2f\en", sum(ints), sum(floats));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
6 0.75
.EE
.RE
.PP
Several type parameters are separated by commas:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
B convert<A, B>(A a, fn(A) B f) {
    return f(a);
}

f64 half(i64 x) {
    return (f64)x / 2.0;
}

i32 main() {
    printf("%.1f\en", convert(7, &half));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
3.5
.EE
.RE
.SH Working out the types
.PP
At a call, ktm works the type parameters out from the arguments: through plain values, pointers, slices, \fBfn\fR types and generic structs. When the arguments disagree, or don't mention a parameter at all, write the types after the name:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
T max<T>(T a, T b) {
    return a > b ? a : b;
}

i32 main() {
    printf("%.1f\en", max<f64>(1, 2.5));
    printf("%d\en", max<i64>(\-3, 2));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
2.5
2
.EE
.RE
.PP
With \fBmax<f64>\fR, the \fB1\fR becomes \fB1.0\fR, as for any \fBf64\fR parameter. A type parameter that only appears in the return type has to be written, since no argument says what it is:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
T zero<T>() {
    T z;
    return z;
}

i32 main() {
    i64 n = zero<i64>();
    f64 f = zero<f64>();
    printf("%d %.1f\en", n, f);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
0 0.0
.EE
.RE
.PP
This does not compile:
.RS 4
.EX
T zero<T>() {
    T z;
    return z;
}

i32 main() {
    i64 n = zero();
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
can't work out the type arguments of `zero`: nothing says what `T` is
.EE
.RE
.SH Generic structs
.PP
A struct can take type parameters too. The type is always written with its arguments, nested ones included: \fBPair<u8 *, f64>\fR, \fBStack<Stack<i64>>\fR\&.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
struct Pair<K, V> {
    K key;
    V value;
};

i32 main() {
    Pair<u8 *, f64> pi = {"pi", 3.14};
    Pair<i64, bool> flag = {7, true};
    printf("%s=%.2f %d=%d\en", pi.key, pi.value, flag.key, flag.value);
    printf("%d %d\en", sizeof(Pair<u8 *, f64>), sizeof(Pair<u8, u8>));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
pi=3.14 7=1
16 2
.EE
.RE
.PP
Each set of arguments is its own struct type, laid out for its types: \fBPair<u8, u8>\fR is 2 bytes.
.PP
A generic function can make and return one; \fBauto\fR keeps the full type:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
struct Pair<K, V> {
    K key;
    V value;
};

Pair<K, V> pairOf<K, V>(K k, V v) {
    Pair<K, V> p = {k, v};
    return p;
}

i32 main() {
    auto e = pairOf("e", 2.72);
    printf("%s=%.2f\en", e.key, e.value);
    printf("%s\en", typeof(e));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
e=2.72
Pair<u8 *, f64>
.EE
.RE
.PP
\fBtypeof(x)\fR is the type of \fBx\fR as text, which helps to see what an instance is.
.PP
A generic struct can hold another generic struct:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
struct Box<T> {
    T value;
};

struct Shelf<T> {
    Box<T> left;
    Box<T> right;
};

i32 main() {
    Shelf<i64> s = {{1}, {2}};
    Box<Box<u8 *>> nested = {{"deep"}};
    printf("%d %d %s\en", s.left.value, s.right.value, nested.value.value);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
1 2 deep
.EE
.RE
.SH Methods of a generic struct
.PP
A method of a generic struct is written with the struct's type parameters after its name, \fBStack<T>.push\fR; inside, \fBT\fR is whatever the instance's \fBT\fR is.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import mem;

struct Stack<T> {
    T *items;
    i64 len;
    i64 cap;
};

void Stack<T>.push(T x) {
    if (self.len == self.cap) {
        self.cap = self.cap ? self.cap * 2 : 4;
        self.items = mem.realloc(self.items, sizeof(T) * self.cap);
    }
    self.items[self.len++] = x;
}

T Stack<T>.pop() {
    return self.items[\-\-self.len];
}

bool Stack<T>.empty() {
    return self.len == 0;
}

void Stack<T>.free() {
    mem.free(self.items);
}

i32 main() {
    scoped Stack<i64> nums;
    for (i64 i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
        nums.push(i * i);
    }
    while (!nums.empty()) {
        printf("%d ", nums.pop());
    }
    printf("\en");
    scoped Stack<u8 *> words;
    words.push("first");
    words.push("second");
    printf("%s\en", words.pop());
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
25 16 9 4 1 
second
.EE
.RE
.PP
Only the methods an instance uses are compiled for it.
.SH Methods with type parameters of their own
.PP
A method can have type parameters of its own, after its name as for a function. It works on a plain struct, and on a generic one next to the struct's parameters:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
struct Counter { i64 n; };

i64 Counter.addAll<V>(V[] xs) {
    for (i64 i = 0; i < xs.len; i++) {
        self.n += (i64)xs[i];
    }
    return self.n;
}

i32 main() {
    Counter c = {0};
    u8 bytes[3] = {1, 2, 3};
    f64 floats[2] = {1.5, 2.5};
    printf("%d\en", c.addAll(bytes));
    printf("%d\en", c.addAll(floats));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
6
9
.EE
.RE
.PP
On a generic struct, a method can map one instance to another:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
struct Pair<T> {
    T a;
    T b;
};

Pair<U> Pair<T>.map<U>(fn(T) U f) {
    Pair<U> p = {f(self.a), f(self.b)};
    return p;
}

f64 half(i64 x) {
    return (f64)x / 2.0;
}

u8 *size(f64 x) {
    return x >= 1.0 ? "big" : "small";
}

i32 main() {
    Pair<i64> p = {1, 5};
    Pair<u8 *> s = p.map(&half).map(&size);
    printf("%s %s\en", s.a, s.b);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
small big
.EE
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
The method's own parameters are worked out from its arguments, as for a function; \fBself\fR takes no part, and the struct's parameters come from the value it is called on.
.IP \(bu 2
Write them when nothing says what they are: \fBc.zero<i64>()\fR for a method whose type parameter is only in its return type.
.IP \(bu 2
Its own parameter names can't repeat the struct's: \fBPair<T>.f<T>\fR is an error.
.IP \(bu 2
Its name is its own: not a field's, not a plain method's, not another generic method's.
.SH Generic functions over generic structs
.PP
A function can take a generic struct and work out its parameters from it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
struct Box<T> {
    T value;
};

T unbox<T>(Box<T> *b) {
    return b.value;
}

void fill<T>(Box<T> *b, T v) {
    b.value = v;
}

i32 main() {
    Box<f64> b = {2.5};
    Box<u8 *> s;
    fill(&s, "packed");
    printf("%.1f %s\en", unbox(&b), unbox(&s));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
2.5 packed
.EE
.RE
.SH A generic base
.PP
A generic struct can extend another, generic or not, and get its methods (see Methods (see ktm-guide-methods(7))):
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
struct List<T> {
    T items[4];
    i64 len;
};

struct Counted<T> : List<T> {
    i64 adds;
};

void List<T>.add(T x) {
    self.items[self.len++] = x;
}

void Counted<T>.addCounted(T x) {
    self.add(x);
    self.adds++;
}

i32 main() {
    Counted<i64> c;
    c.addCounted(10);
    c.addCounted(20);
    printf("%d %d %d %d\en", c.len, c.items[0], c.items[1], c.adds);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
2 10 20 2
.EE
.RE
.SH The library's generic containers
.PP
The containers are generic: \fBvec.Vec<T>\fR, \fBmap.Map<K, V>\fR, \fBset.Set<T>\fR, \fBlist.List<T>\fR (see the library (see ktm-vec(3))). Their constructors take the types:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import map, vec;

i32 main() {
    scoped vec.Vec<u8 *> names = vec.new<u8 *>();
    names.push("ann");
    names.push("bob");
    scoped map.Map<u8 *, i64> ages = map.new<u8 *, i64>();
    ages.set("ann", 41);
    for (i64 i = 0; i < names.len; i++) {
        u8 *n = names.get(i);
        printf("%s %d\en", n, ages.getOr(n, \-1));
    }
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
ann 41
bob \-1
.EE
.RE
.PP
\fBjson.decode<T>\fR is generic too: it decodes into whatever struct \fBT\fR is (see Attributes (see ktm-guide-attributes(7))).
.SH Errors are checked per instance
.PP
A generic body is checked for each set of types it is used with. A body that works for numbers fails for a struct, at the operator that doesn't apply, with a note naming the instance and the call that asked for it:
.PP
This does not compile:
.RS 4
.EX
struct Point { i64 x, y; };

T max<T>(T a, T b) {
    return a > b ? a : b;
}

i32 main() {
    Point a = {1, 2};
    Point b = {3, 4};
    max(a, b);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
max<Point>
.EE
.RE
.PP
There are no constraints on type parameters: the instance either compiles or doesn't.
.SH In modules and libraries
.PP
A generic declared \fBpub\fR in a module is used as \fBmodule.name\fR, like any declaration: \fBcoll.Stack<i64>\fR, \fBcoll.pairOf(1, 2)\fR (see Modules and packages (see ktm-guide-modules-and-packages(7))). A library built with \fBktm \-lib\fR keeps its generics as source, and each program compiles the instances it uses.
.SH Not in ktm
.IP \(bu 2
Constraints on type parameters.
.IP \(bu 2
Generic enums, and numbers as parameters (\fBArray<T, 16>\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
A different body for one type (specialisation).
.IP \(bu 2
Several generic methods of one name, or a generic method next to a plain one of its name.
.IP \(bu 2
The address of a generic method: wrap the call in a function.
.SH Rules
.IP \(bu 2
\fBT name<T>(...)\fR is a generic function; \fBstruct Name<T> { ... }\fR a generic struct; \fBR Name<T>.method(...)\fR a method of one; \fBR Name.method<U>(...)\fR a method with its own type parameters.
.IP \(bu 2
Each set of type arguments is compiled into its own instance, when first used.
.IP \(bu 2
A call works the types out from the arguments; write \fBf<T>(...)\fR when it can't.
.IP \(bu 2
A generic struct type is always written with its arguments.
.IP \(bu 2
The body is checked per instance; there are no constraints.
